<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yumjaagiin Tsedenbal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yumjaagiin Tsedenbal was the leader of the Mongolian People's Republic for over three decades, as head of the ruling party from 1940 to 1954 and from 1958 to 1984, and as prime minister from 1952 to 1974 and then head of state. Closely tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, he oversaw collectivisation, industrialisation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>'s admission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1961. He was removed in 1984 and spent his last years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yumjaagiin-tsedenbal.jpg" alt="Yumjaagiin Tsedenbal" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altered_portrait_of_Yumjaagiin_Tsedenbal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yumjaagiin Tsedenbal, born Tserenpil, was born on 17 September 1916 to an unwed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorbet">Dörbet</a> mother in a banner of what is now Uvs Province. In October 1929 he and 21 other Mongolian students were chosen for a special preparatory school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irkutsk">Irkutsk</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and in 1931 he joined the Mongolian Revolutionary Youth League. He graduated from the Siberian Financial and Economic Institute in July 1938, was noticed by the Soviet Communist Party's Central Committee on a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> that September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Recommended to the leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorloogiin-choibalsan">Khorloogiin Choibalsan</a> by a Soviet intelligence officer, Tsedenbal joined the party in 1939 and became finance minister. In March 1940, aged 24, he was elected general secretary, the country's number-two leader, after Choibalsan had the sitting secretary Banzarjavyn Baasanjav purged; Baasanjav was executed and rehabilitated in 1957. Tsedenbal introduced the Cyrillic script for Mongolian. After Choibalsan died in January 1952 he became premier on 27 May, and in 1958 he ousted Dashiin Damba, who had held the party post since 1954, and exiled him internally.</p><p>He resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-stalinization">de-Stalinization</a>: he blocked the commission re-examining the purges, banned the film Tümnii Neg in 1963, and a statue of Stalin stood before the National Library until 1990. He expelled rivals on charges of nationalism, among them Daramyn Tömör-Ochir in 1962, after the planned celebration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s 800th anniversary was cancelled, and in 1964 an &quot;anti-party group&quot; of three economic officials. He called the Soviet relationship one of elder and younger brother, sided with the USSR in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split">Sino-Soviet split</a>, and signed a treaty in 1966 that allowed Soviet troops to be stationed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. He built a cult of personality, awarding himself the rank of marshal in 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Memory loss and dizziness troubled him from late 1973. He resigned as premier in 1974, becoming head of state. In November 1983 the new Soviet leadership judged his behaviour a liability, and on 23 August 1984 the Mongolian Politburo dismissed him from all positions, citing his health. He lived in retirement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where he died on 20 April 1991.</p><p>In 1988 the official media began blaming him by name for the country's stagnation, and in March 1990, at the height of the democratic revolution, the party stripped him of his titles and expelled him. A decree of President Punsalmaagiin Ochirbat in 1997 invalidated that decision. A statue of him was built in 2000, and his legacy, controversial after 1990, is remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> as one of relatively moderate socialism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yumjaagiin Tsedenbal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cyrillic alphabet was adopted in the 1940s, and under <b>Yumjaagiin Tsedenbal</b> from 1952 Mongolia industrialised and joined the United Nations in 1961.</blockquote><a href="/state/mongolian-peoples-republic">Mongolian People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Uvs Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.5,92.5&amp;year=1964" aria-label="Uvs Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6678 439 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yumjaagiin Tsedenbal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7128 709h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7128 709h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.5,92.5&amp;year=1964">What was here in 1964? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
